A||||9||Cozmo||Steering wheel radio controls question||||5||
Z||000000||Cozmo||08-22-2001||07:00 AM||||I just got most of a Delco premium sound system from a '89 Bonneville from the local salvage yard.  It is a Delco-Loc cassette head unit with radio controls in the steering wheel.  I tried to snag every part possibly needed to modify it to fit in a Fiero. I got the..... <BR>...Head unit- orange backlighting but a BLUE display (did they ever make a red display?)<BR>...Steering wheel and all the column wiring and switches etc. (the wheel needs recovering but besides that it's in fine shape).<BR>...Whole speaker system. Some very nice drivers that look like they will adapt to a Fiero.<P>OK I got the whole mess back to the shop and started tinkering with it.  The head has a standard configuration 20 pin single piece GM connector.  It also has a 14 pin connector with 3 wires in it and a standard antenna connector.  I hooked it up to a battery and speakers, turned it on and it powered up but said "Loc" on the display.<BR>Oh Oh...<BR>I read the front of the radio, it says "Delco-Loc".  Double Oh-Oh <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>Did some research, GM made 2 security systems for their radios, "Delco-Loc" and Delco-Loc II".  This was not a II, thank goodness as they look like a real pain if you don't have the access codes or the VIN of the donor car. As far as the original security system all you need is the original steering controls hooked up and it will work.<BR>So this was encouraging. Looking at the wheel controls, there are 5 wires coming out of the switch panel that go through 4 slip rings and a bunch of active electronics in the column to some connectors.  Seeing the number of controls and the fact that there are only 5 wires tells me it must have some sort of data bus (The wheel also controls the climate control stuff).<BR>I figured out that 3 of the wires to the wheel assembly were 12V, ground and dash lights.  That left 2 "mystery wires".  There were 3 "mystery wires on the radio...Hmmmm?<BR>So I hooked all the power, ground and dashlights up and powered it up. No magic smoke, everything lit up but it still said "Loc".  Now the test.  The two unknown wires from the wheel go to a 40 pin IC with a proprietary part # on it. (oh well, can't look that up...).  Of the 3 unknown radio wires, they all go to a small PCB in the radio. (I didn't tear it apart to examine it any further).<BR>I decided to try connecting what looked like the data lines together and VOILA the sucker worked!!!<BR>All the radio controls in the wheel now control the radio, and it lights up red too!<BR>Here's my questions...<BR>I plan to use a Fiero column with cruise and intermittent wipers. I'd like to add the wheel and "slip ring" from the donor car.  By connecting the dash light wire to 12V, I only need to pass 4 lines through the slip ring.  Then all I'd need to do is snake 4 more wires down the column to the radio.<BR>If I could, I'd like to use the electronic climate controls on the wheel too.  I know the Fiero has some electronic control for that, could I possibly use the panel from the donor and make it work? (the panel is a little wider, but it can be "modded").<P>I test fit the wheel on my '87 Coupe and it fits perfect and looks great.  Even tho the radio is only a cassette, it has full electronic controls, equalizer and a HUGE power amp heat sink.  The whole system looks right at home in a Fiero. I'll post some pics when I get my digital camera back. <BR>These radios are somewhat common at the yards, and pretty cheap (where I go it's $5 for the wheel, $10 for the steering column "guts" and $10 for the radio).<BR>Anybody tried any of this with success?<P>BobC<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||08-22-2001||09:59 AM||||Sounds nice, Bob...<P>From what I have read the climate control thing will be tricky. Apparently it is much more than just changing the head and stuff.<P>Maybe some others will know more, but I think if you do a search on climate control or something like that you may find some more info.<P>Look forward to seeing your pictures!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
